## 5. PHASE THREE: TECHNICAL SEO AUDIT

Perform a complete technical SEO inspection.

### A. Crawlability

Verify whether important public pages can be accessed by search engine crawlers.

Check:

- HTTP response status
- Unexpected redirects
- Accidental 404 and 5xx errors
- robots.txt restrictions
- Meta robots directives
- X-Robots-Tag headers where applicable
- Blocked JavaScript, CSS, or important assets
- Crawlable internal links
- Login restrictions affecting public pages
- Hosting or CDN rules that may block crawlers

Do not block important public pages.

Do not remove legitimate protections from private pages or APIs.

### B. Indexability

Inspect:

- Whether important pages are intended to be indexed
- Accidental noindex directives
- Conflicting robots directives
- Canonical tags
- Duplicate URLs
- HTTP versus HTTPS variants
- www versus non-www variants, if applicable
- Trailing-slash inconsistencies
- Query-parameter duplicates
- Redirect chains
- Soft 404 risks
- Orphaned pages

Select the canonical URL based on the site's actual deployed configuration.

Do not blindly force a URL format that breaks the existing hosting setup.

Where necessary, implement appropriate redirects and canonical tags.

Do not redirect unrelated URLs to the homepage.

### C. XML sitemap

Inspect the existing sitemap.

If one is needed, generate a valid XML sitemap containing only canonical, public, indexable URLs that should appear in search.

Requirements:

- Use absolute URLs.
- Use the correct deployed domain.
- Exclude noindex pages.
- Exclude redirecting URLs.
- Exclude error pages.
- Exclude private pages and API endpoints.
- Avoid duplicate URLs.
- Use accurate last-modified dates when available.
- Do not invent future modification dates.
- Do not include URLs that do not exist.

Ensure the sitemap can be accessed at the expected location, usually:

https://xer0byte.netlify.app/sitemap.xml

If the deployed website has a different canonical domain, adapt accordingly.

### D. robots.txt

Inspect or create a valid robots.txt.

Allow crawling of important public content and assets.

Do not accidentally block the entire website.

Do not use robots.txt as a substitute for noindex directives.

Include the sitemap location if appropriate.

Verify the final file after the production build.

### E. HTTP and HTTPS

Verify that the public site uses HTTPS.

Check for:

- Mixed content
- Invalid certificate assumptions
- Unnecessary redirect hops
- Broken canonical URLs
- Inconsistent internal links

Do not claim to have verified the live HTTPS configuration unless the deployed site was actually tested.

### F. JavaScript SEO

If the site uses JavaScript rendering:

- Check whether important content is discoverable by search engines.
- Ensure meaningful text is available in rendered HTML.
- Ensure internal navigation uses crawlable links.
- Avoid hiding essential content behind user interactions.
- Check whether metadata changes correctly between routes.
- Avoid relying on a single static document title for every page.
- Check whether client-side routing creates indexing or canonicalization problems.

If the site is a single-page application, assess whether its current rendering architecture is sufficient.

Do not migrate frameworks simply because server-side rendering is available.

Recommend a framework or rendering change only if a verified limitation justifies the cost and complexity.

